  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; full paper text is present",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "Applies weak-teacher distillation to visual learning tasks and reports training outcomes, extending the setting beyond language. Its protocol differs from weak-label-only fine-tuning: it adds teacher distillation to task training that also uses ground-truth labels.",
    "gate_note": "The paper evaluates acceleration to target quality, not Burns et al.'s PGR. The student uses the ordinary task loss on labelled data plus a teacher-distillation loss; it is not trained only on labels produced by the weaker model. Table 1 and Table 2 time-to-target results and Table 3 teacher-mismatch results are recorded. Method-specific ablations and representation diagnostics do not measure a listed claim version and are omitted.",
    "test_reason": "Not a test. The student trains on ground-truth labels (L_base) plus an early, scheduled distillation term from a weaker frozen teacher. Distillation is switched off once the student surpasses the teacher. The measure is epochs or steps to reach a target metric, compared with label-only training. No student is trained on weak labels alone, and no PGR or weak-vs-strong comparison under weak supervision is reported. Beating the teacher is guaranteed by the ground-truth labels, so the results cannot bear on V1 or V6. This is a related construct (training acceleration by auxiliary weak-teacher KD). Burns et al. are cited only as framing.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors conclude that early-only distillation from a suitably weaker frozen teacher, added to standard supervised training and stopped after the student surpasses the teacher, reduces epochs or steps to target across classification, detection and diffusion. Final accuracy stays near baseline. Too-weak or too-strong teachers reduce gains, and the best teacher band varies by task, model and optimizer.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses a frozen weaker teacher and early scheduled distillation, but combines teacher outputs with the ordinary task loss on labelled training data and measures time-to-target rather than weak-label PGR.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Acceleration varies with teacher strength/checkpoint stage; the authors report the strongest gains for suitably weaker teachers and reduced or reversed gains for mismatch settings.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Adding early KD from a moderately weaker frozen teacher to ground-truth training reaches target metrics sooner. ImageNet speedups run up to 4.75×, COCO 1.67×, and CIFAR-10 diffusion 2.67×. Too-weak teachers can slow training (0.51×, 0.80×), and too-strong teachers give near parity.",
        "proposed_claim": "Auxiliary distillation from a suitably weaker teacher accelerates supervised visual training, with an optimal teacher-strength band.",
